\nSo every non-zero offset gets flipped:\n- `parse_tzinfo(\"+05:30\")` gives UTC−05:30.\n- `parse_datetime(\"2020-01-01T10:00:00+05:00\")` comes back as `...-05:00`.\n- Parsing and re-formatting any offset timestamp turns `+` into `-` and the other way round.\n\n`Z` and naive (no time zone) strings are unaffected. All 35 failures come from this one bug: time and datetime parsing with offsets, `parse_tzinfo`, round trips, and comparisons against the standard library's `datetime.fromisoformat`. To confirm, I ran the suite against a temporary copy of the library with just that one character flipped, and all 380 tests passed. The repo itself was not touched.\n\nI left these tests failing on purpose. Making them pass without fixing the library would mean asserting that `+05:00` means UTC−5, which would lock the bug in. If you'd like, I can fix line 33 so the full suite goes green.\n\n**What the suite covers:**\n- **Dates:** every parse format (basic, extended, 6-digit expanded, ordinal, week dates including year-boundary weeks, reduced precision, default month and day), invalid and out-of-range input, formatting, and round trips.\n- **Times:** fractions with `.` or `,`, truncation rather than rounding of extra digits, time zone variants, and formatting.\n- **Time zones:** `FixedOffset`, `UTC` and its pickling, the local zone, `tz_isoformat` in all three formats plus its error cases.\n- **Durations:** parsing standard, signed, fractional and alternative-format durations, formatting, and week format.\n- **`Duration` class:** equality, hashing, arithmetic, adding to and subtracting from dates (end-of-month and leap-year clamping), `totimedelta`, and pickling.\n- **Helpers:** `fquotmod`, `max_days_in_month`, and the public exports.\n\n**Expectations I dropped:** three of mine turned out to be intended behaviour, so I adjusted those tests rather than report them as bugs:\n- A month of `00` falls back to the default month.\n- `PT` and `P1YT` are accepted, since the parser is lenient by design.\n- Week-date formatting uses the calendar year, so dates near a year boundary don't round-trip.\n\nRunning the tests also created an untracked `isodate/__pycache__/` folder.","ttft_ms":2618,"type":"result","duration_ms":182573,"uuid":"33b1bcbb-0e5e-4871-a556-582c790352a0","ttft_stream_ms":1439,"time_to_request_ms":589,"first_content_frame_ms":1439,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
